## Installing

Inside TYPO3 there is nothing to install. Every release ships a pinned version of the set and registers it with the icon registry; the ViewHelper and the web component resolve identifiers out of the box.

Outside TYPO3 — a standalone project, a style guide, a build step of your own — the set is a package of plain assets with no runtime attached:

```bash
npm install @typo3/icons
```

| Path | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `dist/icons.css` | The component styles: sizes, states, overlays, spinning. |
| `dist/icons.scss` | The same, as source, if you compile your own. |
| `dist/svgs/` | One file per icon, by category. |
| `dist/sprites/` | One sprite per category, for `<use>`. |
| `dist/scss/` | Per category Sass variables holding each icon as a data URI. |
| `dist/icons.json` | Every identifier with its category and file paths, for tooling. |

## Placing an icon

Four routes to the same drawing. They differ in what resolves the identifier — the Core, the browser, or your stylesheet — not in what ends up on screen.

### In a Fluid template

The ViewHelper the Core registers. It writes the markup, the overlay and the size class for you.

```html
<core:icon identifier="actions-document-edit" size="small" />
<core:icon identifier="apps-pagetree-page" overlay="overlay-hidden" size="medium" />
```

### In JavaScript or plain HTML

A custom element that fetches the sprite itself, for markup assembled in the browser.

```html
<typo3-backend-icon identifier="actions-document-edit" size="small"></typo3-backend-icon>
```

### From the SVG sprite

The markup the two above produce. One request per category, and the glyph inherits `currentColor` like any text around it.

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="node_modules/@typo3/icons/dist/icons.css">

<span class="icon icon-size-small">
    <span class="icon-markup">
        <svg role="img"><use xlink:href="dist/sprites/actions.svg#actions-document-edit" /></svg>
    </span>
</span>
```

### From a stylesheet

No markup at all: each icon is a Sass variable holding the drawing as a data URI. The mark cannot inherit a colour this way, so this route is for the cases where the colour is fixed anyway.

```scss
@use "@typo3/icons/dist/icons" as *;
@use "@typo3/icons/dist/scss/icons-variables-actions" as *;

.myclass {
    background-size: 1em 1em;
    background-image: $icon-actions-document-edit;
}
```

## Classes

From `dist/icons.css`. The wrapper carries the size and the colour, the layers inside carry the drawing.

| Class | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `icon` | The wrapper. One em square by default, in `currentColor`. |
| `icon-markup` | The layer the glyph sits in. Required. |
| `icon-overlay` | A second glyph, at 68.75% in the bottom corner. |
| `icon-size-small` | 16px. Also `icon-size-medium` 32, `icon-size-large` 48, `icon-size-mega` 64. Without one, the icon is as big as the text it sits in. |
| `icon-spin` | Turns the markup layer, two seconds a revolution. |
| `icon-bidi` | Flips the drawing in a right-to-left context. |
| `icon-state-disabled` | Fades the markup layer to the disabled opacity. |

## Custom properties

The wrapper reads these, so a size or a colour can be set from anywhere above it without touching the markup.

```css
.myclass {
    --icon-size: 20px;
    --icon-color-primary: currentColor;
    --icon-color-accent: #79a548;
    --icon-opacity-disabled: .5;
}
```

`--icon-color-accent` is the one a backend module icon paints its highlight with, and the only colour in the set that is not `currentColor`.

## How the drawings are built

{% for grid, count in grids %}- {{ count }} icon{% if count != 1 %}s{% endif %} on a {{ grid }} by {{ grid }} grid.{{ "\n" }}{% endfor %}

Most of the set is drawn at 16 pixels. Overlays are drawn smaller so they stay legible in the corner of another icon, and backend module icons are drawn at 64 because the module menu renders them large.

